kind: translation
rules: model-role, invoke
intent: Points the missing-setup check at the project models file, which is per project rather than per user. Keep the verify-skill bullet unchanged. The state check names the setup-pstack skill; the skill to mention to the user gets its Codex form.
--- upstream
- No `~/.cursor/rules/pstack-models.mdc` means `/setup-pstack` hasn't run for this user, so every role uses its default model.
- No `verify-*` skill or other app harness in the project means agents have no scripted way to drive the app. Mention `/create-verification-skill` when the question is about proving a change works.
--- patched
- No {{snippet:models-file}} means the **setup-pstack** skill hasn't run for this project, so every role uses its default model.
- No `verify-*` skill or other app harness in the project means agents have no scripted way to drive the app. Mention `/create-verification-skill` (`$create-verification-skill` in Codex) when the question is about proving a change works.

=== entry 9438e5e84262
kind: translation
rules: delegate, loop, cursor-only, invoke
intent: Says pstack is built for T3 Code and that its subagents and scheduled tasks are T3 features; Custom Modes are dropped. No rule covers the host statement itself, so this is the closest faithful version. Keep the point that other tools can read the skills but the subagent parts may not work there. The example skills are named, not typed.
--- upstream
pstack is built for Cursor. Its skills use the Agent Skills format, so other tools can read them. But most workflow skills, including `/poteto-mode`, `/how`, `/why`, and `/teach`, spawn Cursor subagents with per-role models, and Custom Modes and `/loop` are Cursor features, so those parts may not work there.
--- patched
pstack is built for T3 Code, running Claude Code or Codex. Its skills use the Agent Skills format, so other tools can read them. But most workflow skills, including **poteto-mode**, **how**, **why**, and **teach**, spawn T3 child tasks with per-role models, and scheduled tasks are a T3 feature, so those parts may not work there.

=== entry 6bdd7aadac18
kind: translation
rules: todolist, unshipped-link, invoke
intent: Names where the playbook steps are kept in T3: `update_plan` in Codex, the reply in Claude Code, and drops the link to guide page 2, which isn't installed. Keep the skip marker and the prompting advice. The opening sentence describes the skill, so it names it rather than giving a command.
--- upstream
`/poteto-mode` matches the task to a playbook, copies the playbook's steps into the todo list, and runs the other skills as the steps need them. A step it skips stays in the list as `skip: <reason>`. A good prompt states the goal and how to tell it's done. It doesn't list skills, because a hand-written sequence tends to drop or reorder steps the playbook would keep. Read [`references/prompting.md`](references/prompting.md) before you help word one. [Guide page 2](../../docs/guide/02-poteto-mode.md) has examples.
--- patched
The **poteto-mode** skill matches the task to a playbook, copies the playbook's steps into a visible step list (`update_plan` in Codex, the reply in Claude Code), and runs the other skills as the steps need them. A step it skips stays in the list as `skip: <reason>`. A good prompt states the goal and how to tell it's done. It doesn't list skills, because a hand-written sequence tends to drop or reorder steps the playbook would keep. Read [`references/prompting.md`](references/prompting.md) before you help word one.

=== entry f44c92abb350
kind: translation
rules: cursor-only, invoke
intent: Drops Custom Modes (the keyboard shortcut, Use as Mode, and where Cursor lists them); what is left is that an invocation covers one message, so start each task with the skill. Keep the fade warning.
--- upstream
- Enter on `/poteto-mode` attaches the skill to one message. It fades as the chat moves on.
- Option+Enter on Mac or Alt+Enter on Windows, or Use as Mode from the skill entry, makes it a Custom Mode. It stays in context every turn until the user exits the mode, and it stays out of casual turns.
- Cursor's docs list Custom Modes in the Agents Window and the CLI. Elsewhere, start each new task with `/poteto-mode`.
--- patched
- Enter on `/poteto-mode` (`$poteto-mode` in Codex) attaches the skill to one message. It fades as the chat moves on.
- To keep it on, start each new task with `/poteto-mode`.

=== entry 7cfdf24a926d
kind: translation
rules: cursor-only, agent-brief, invoke
intent: Drops the link to Cursor's skills docs, and says a poteto-agent subagent comes from its brief file, not an agent type. Keep the "new task" note and that poteto-mode already uses poteto-agent. The skill is named, not typed.
--- upstream
Link [Cursor's skills docs](https://cursor.com/docs/skills) when this comes up. Mid-chat, "new task" makes the mode match a fresh playbook. `/poteto-mode` already uses `poteto-agent` for the subagents its playbook steps spawn. To get the same style from a subagent of your own, spawn it with `subagent_type: "poteto-agent"`.
--- patched
Mid-chat, "new task" makes the mode match a fresh playbook. The **poteto-mode** skill already uses `poteto-agent` for the subagents its playbook steps spawn. To get the same style from a subagent of your own, start its brief with "Read `<abs>/pstack/agents/poteto-agent.md` and act as that agent. The poteto-mode skill is at `<abs>/poteto-mode/SKILL.md`.", where `<abs>` is the absolute path of the folder that holds the pstack skills (resolve `..` from this skill's folder).

